Thanks for letting me tag along last night (Sunday 7/8/01) it was great.  I got to see some places I've never seen before and monsters that I would never see if I were on my own.  Kinda an interesting place with all the ice on the floor and lots of wide open spaces to kill yourself when you fall.<P>What went wrong?  Near as I can tell we had this huge train dropped on our Arses.  I watched a minimum of six MObs run past us and the last few decided to pick on our party.  I was just about to log out for the night so I'd forgotten the evac spell and had a port spell in it's place.  Nef was down before I could blink, one attacked me while trying to cast a heal spell.  I took a header off the balcony, survived the fall and cast port with a few HP left.<BR>Afterword I'd felt really bad that I had bugged out.<P>Anyway I was thinking about it for a while today and I thought about how tight the Alpha group seems to have become.  There are even some posts here that run along those lines.  Everyone knows what everyone else is doing, without all that extra chatter.<BR>However, and I hate to say it, I need that extra chatter.  A group needs one leader.  That leader needs to spell it out for me.  "Follow me."  "Invis Nef", "Make sure to have Evac memmed."  You are in charge of healing, you are the backup healer, you buff etc.<BR>Most of my group experience has been with 'slap together' groups.  Sometimes it works, most of the time it doesn't.  When it does work there has been a strong leader.  A leader who knows what all the different classes can and can not do.  I believe that any one of you guys could be a great leader but when you are always playing with other great group players things get kinda loose.  Reasonable so of course, I'm just pointing out that I don't think I'm such a great group player right now.<P>Also, the longest amount of time spent in a group is getting everyone together and then finding a location.  Every time you change locations it will take a minimum of a half hour.  EVEN if that is just over the hill.  Moral of the story is that if you are only going to play for another hour or so, stick with where you are.  The experinece might not be great but it beats a half hour downtime.<P>Just some thoughts.
</FONT>
